  "id": "Compound::DB00777 - Gene::1133",
  "verbalization": "Propiomazine is likely to interact with Chrm5 through a mechanism involving G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptors. The evidence suggests that Propiomazine binds to Chrm1 or Chrm2, which in turn participate in the activity of G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptors. These receptors are known to be involved in the activation of Chrm5, indicating a potential pathway where Propiomazine modulates the activity of Chrm5 via its interaction with these receptors. The high scores in the paths suggest a strong and consistent relationship between Propiomazine and Chrm5, supported by the shared involvement of G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptors in the activation of Chrm5. The mechanism appears to be mediated through the G-protein pathway, with Propiomazine acting as a ligand that influences the function of Chrm5 through its interaction with these receptors.",
